<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Union of Brest</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1595">1595</a>–<a href="/years/1596">1596</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Union of Brest was the agreement of 1595-1596 by which most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> accepted the authority of the pope while keeping their Byzantine rite and customs. Confirmed at a synod in Brest in 1596, it created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenian-uniate-church">Ruthenian Uniate Church</a>. It split the Orthodox population and caused lasting religious conflict that fed later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> revolts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/union-of-brest.jpg" alt="Union of Brest" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathiasrex on layers of Halibutt; derivative work by Hoodinski · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Religie_w_I_Rz-plitej_1573.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Rome-oriented and Byzantium-oriented Christians had formally severed connections from 1054, and several attempts at reunion followed, among them the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> of 1439, which the deposed Metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, Isidore, endorsed. In 1588 and 1589 the Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, Jeremias II, travelled through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> and the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> he recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> and consecrated Job as Patriarch. In the Commonwealth he deposed the Metropolitan of Kiev, Onesiphorus Divochka, and, with the approval of King Sigismund III, consecrated Michael Rohoza as the new Metropolitan of Kiev, Halych and all Rus'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After Jeremias II left, some of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> bishops of the Ruthenian lands gathered in synod at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-france">Brest</a> and signed a declaration of readiness to reunite with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Several others joined later, among them Hypatius Pociej, Bishop of Volodymyr-Brest, and Michael Rohoza, while Hedeon Balaban and Mykhailo Kopystenskyi withdrew their signatures in 1595. Prince Konstanty Wasyl Ostrogski held that the Metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> should first reach agreement with the eastern patriarchs.</p><p>The union was proclaimed in the Hall of Constantine in the Vatican. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' letter to the pope, dated 12 June 1595, was read in Ruthenian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Pociej and Bishop Cyril Terlecki of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutsk">Lutsk</a> read the formula abjuring the schism of 1054, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-viii">Pope Clement VIII</a> promised his support. The bull Magnus Dominus et laudabilis nimis announced the union, and on 7 February 1596 the pope called a synod of the Ruthenian bishops.</p><p>The 33 articles of union were accepted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenians">Ruthenians</a> kept their own rite, apart from customs opposed to Catholic doctrine. It was agreed that the filioque would not be inserted into their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> asked to be excused from introducing the Gregorian Calendar, to avoid popular discontent, and asked that the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> grant them the dignity of senators by right. A bull of 23 February 1596, Decet Romanum pontificem, defined the rights of the Ruthenian episcopate and their subjection to the Holy See.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a> strongly supported the union. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, prominent nobles led by Ostrogski, and the nascent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> movement for Ukrainian self-rule opposed it, and the Church of Rus' split into Greek Catholic and Greek Orthodox jurisdictions. Enforcement on Orthodox parishes provoked several large uprisings, and the union lost some of its early support within decades. In 1620 a Metropolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and all Ruthenia was set up under the Patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> for dissenting Orthodox, which produced parallel successions of metropolitans in the Commonwealth. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenian-uniate-church">Ruthenian Uniate Church</a> survives as the Ukrainian and Belarusian Greek Catholic Churches.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Union of Brest” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Union of Brest</b> of 1596, which created the Ruthenian Uniate Church, and the long coexistence of Catholics, Orthodox, Protestants, Jews, Tatars and Karaites made the grand duchy one of Europe's most religiously diverse states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1596 the <b>Union of Brest</b> brought the Ruthenian bishops into communion with Rome.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
